		{"id":"dbc0609792900850","type":"text","text":"Unity keywords:\n* Shader graph\n* URP\n* SRP\n* Lit, unlit\n* ShaderLab\n* Material\n* Shaders","x":-3740,"y":540,"width":386,"height":300},
		{"id":"8ec2bc10404f64ef","type":"text","text":"Material modeling in computer graphics","x":-788,"y":868,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"5ca7933efde182ab","type":"text","text":"Scattering function","x":-1035,"y":1060,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"99475e8f0aa31334","type":"text","text":"Volumes to mesh for complex structures like terrains\n\n","x":-1480,"y":3127,"width":354,"height":104},
		{"id":"664d2c5068d0206f","type":"text","text":"As modeling tool for shape and animation\n\nModeling not only surface, but interior (volume) of objects:\n*  Medical, scientific visualizations, 3D printing, film production\n\nObject interior can be assigned with data used for modeling operations, e.g., vector fields:\n* https://www.openvdb.org/documentation/\n","x":-1400,"y":1973,"width":780,"height":279,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"8d1193a66caeef79","type":"text","text":"Texture","x":-609,"y":1060,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"81ef840edda737bd","type":"text","text":"3D","x":-474,"y":1236,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"5b5f02a8e984ff34","type":"text","text":"2D","x":-775,"y":1241,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"23deec10b37eb4fc","type":"text","text":"Great in conjuction with other shape representations for modeling","x":-2098,"y":2635,"width":373,"height":92},
		{"id":"eabbc5da2383f4dc","type":"text","text":"Volumes and SDF","x":-2037,"y":2930,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"25e4be2fb113f8a5","type":"text","text":"Mesh to volume for volumetric sampling and scattering\n\n* Polygonal mesh -> voxels (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/215506131_A_Fast_Depth-Buffer-Based_Voxelization_Algorithm)\n* GPU compute shaders are useful tool for fast voxelization (https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/1882261.1866201)","x":-1911,"y":3116,"width":396,"height":300},
		{"id":"282a93e5faca366c","type":"text","text":"Volumes and Polygons","x":-1640,"y":2930,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"aea0ce9dc948e681","type":"text","text":"# Procedural material modeling: Material structures","x":-7260,"y":15,"width":1020,"height":104},
		{"id":"5caf7a22afaffeb6","type":"text","text":"Lovro Bosnar","x":-7260,"y":161,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"7fb315c91ae17d43","type":"text","text":"https://graphics.pixar.com/library/ElementalAir/paper.pdf","x":-5000,"y":676,"width":401,"height":76},
		{"id":"3fa1b18a4d7bad5e","type":"file","file":"Images/Material/Examples/RealTimeVolumes_HorizonZeroDawn.jpg","x":-5000,"y":848,"width":851,"height":479},
		{"id":"b7de60e74e144adb","type":"text","text":"https://www.guerrilla-games.com/read/the-real-time-volumetric-cloudscapes-of-horizon-zero-dawn","x":-5000,"y":1327,"width":851,"height":71},
		{"id":"1ac4fa8db85573f1","type":"text","text":"General staging in Houdini:\n* https://www.sidefx.com/tutorials/houdini-for-the-new-artist-2/","x":4140,"y":1420,"width":634,"height":128},
		{"id":"e5b60b7e427b03c6","type":"text","text":"Note: material modeling in Houdini is performed using \"Stage\" network, which contains LOPs: materials (MaterialX), lights, cameras and renderers. Procedural textures in Houdini are created using COPs which are then used in material modeling using \"op:\" https://www.sidefx.com/docs/houdini/copernicus/working_with_cops.html#bringing-cops-into-lops-for-materials -- it seems that apprentice licence is not allowing this to work since image textures are limited.","x":3400,"y":1650,"width":674,"height":220},
		{"id":"d68dca55ea3e5661","type":"text","text":"Dielectrics:\n* Reflection + refraction","x":-1039,"y":-555,"width":250,"height":99},
		{"id":"bcecf3ec4237f099","type":"text","text":"Surfaces","x":-1021,"y":195,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"8557312b9883cdb8","type":"text","text":"Glossy","x":-851,"y":506,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"b22165cfc2f8e63b","type":"link","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z7y_bJSdMgg","x":5440,"y":-646,"width":640,"height":360},
		{"id":"dd0a27ff79cf0b53","type":"text","text":"3. Mesh to volume operator","x":7906,"y":561,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"193f31d5e5897480","type":"text","text":"1. Base shape using mesh","x":7906,"y":-208,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"9bd84fdd26702a87","type":"file","file":"Images/Material/LectureExamples/2/r4.jpg","x":7743,"y":-788,"width":400,"height":225},
		{"id":"98eea47a2826ffa4","type":"text","text":"General tutorials on Copernicus:\n* https://www.sidefx.com/learn/copernicus/\n* https://www.sidefx.com/tutorials/the-ultimate-copernicus-guide/","x":4140,"y":1600,"width":634,"height":320},
		{"id":"df8620c6b1681694","type":"text","text":"Practical material modeling examples in Houdini:\n* Staging, MaterialX, COPs, Karma: https://www.sidefx.com/tutorials/how-to-make-a-sponge-material-in-houdini-materialx-copernicus-workflow/\n* https://www.sidefx.com/tutorials/houdini-205-copernicus-creating-a-sharp-rock-material/\n* https://www.sidefx.com/tutorials/houdini-designer-substance-designer-done-the-houdini-way/\n* https://www.sidefx.com/tutorials/how-to-create-organic-textures/\n* https://www.sidefx.com/tutorials/realistic-shading-using-cops-and-karma-xpu/","x":4140,"y":1960,"width":634,"height":384},
		{"id":"37635f0845fbff5e","type":"text","text":"Blender material modeling","x":3240,"y":-478,"width":1480,"height":1154},
		{"id":"f9f0549484f12323","type":"text","text":"Metals:\n* Only reflection","x":-539,"y":-555,"width":250,"height":99},
		{"id":"b64fc9196db0946f","type":"text","text":"Hair/fur modeling using curves and animation using volume vector fields","x":-1080,"y":3107,"width":250,"height":100},
		{"id":"e4ce225639073628","type":"text","text":"Images and voxels\n\n* Set of images -> voxels (https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/2492045.2492053)\n* Set of images (slices) -> voxels (https://inria.hal.science/inria-00510221v1/document)","x":-797,"y":2934,"width":397,"height":223},
		{"id":"635368b941a59a2e","type":"text","text":"Volumes and curves","x":-1080,"y":2930,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"647fc0f538def202","type":"text","text":"Volumes and points\n\n* Scanning devices -> data points in space (point cloud) -> voxels (https://www.taylorfrancis.com/chapters/edit/10.1201/9781351052108-18/octree-mapping-depth-camera-dave-kotfis)","x":-290,"y":2942,"width":310,"height":232},
		{"id":"50d7d27b7f74e8a4","type":"text","text":"Material","x":-2520,"y":27,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"d270f65edf642520","type":"text","text":"Material modeling is process of specifying optical material properties for geometry.\n\nThese material properties are used during rendering with information on geometry, light and camera to produce colors of objects.\n\nMaterial modeling is done in \"Staging\" part of modeling.","x":-3080,"y":340,"width":655,"height":196},
		{"id":"9577ee5688b585ca","type":"text","text":"* Procedural natural materials:\n\n\t* Procedural fur and hair\n\n\t\t  * Procedural feathers (e.g., bird feather)\n\n\t\t  * Procedural scales (e.g., fish scales)\n\n\t\t  * Procedural porous materials (e.g., sponge)\n\n\t\t  * Foliage patterns","x":277,"y":1002,"width":592,"height":416},
		{"id":"e74d0c4023d50b4c","type":"text","text":"* Procedural industrial materials:\n\n  * Procedural textile (e.g., cloth)\n\n  * Procedural glass (e.g., frosted glass)","x":1055,"y":1012,"width":514,"height":366},
		{"id":"fd5d98c3a726266c","type":"text","text":"Topics and concepts:\n\n\n* Optical material properties modeling and theory\n\n* Mechanical material properties modeling and theory\n\n* Material scales and observation distance: micro, meso and macro\n\n* Material vs geometry\n\n* Material scales in practice: scattering equation, texture and geometry\n\n* Material and light in theory and practice\n\n* Procedural texture: a function\n\n* Procedural textures: surface vs volume\n\n* Procedural textures for material surface or volume variation\n---\n\n\n---\n\n","x":589,"y":-239,"width":830,"height":1097},
		{"id":"abdbecc31cb7b76b","type":"file","file":"Images/Material/Tools/SSS_Statue2.jpg","x":-3241,"y":-2292,"width":266,"height":258},
		{"id":"8d9034d2bc6f6d25","type":"text","text":"Diffuse","x":-1452,"y":508,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"c8686ffbb712ada2","type":"text","text":"Transmissive","x":-551,"y":506,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"485ef197df485629","type":"text","text":"Volumes","x":-301,"y":195,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"25bd6484482ca06d","type":"text","text":"SSS","x":-251,"y":506,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"b5584a2161a8a663","type":"text","text":"Transparent","x":-1039,"y":-299,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"68ea8ab4dcfaab82","type":"text","text":"SSS","x":-734,"y":-299,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"38675b18374df506","type":"text","text":"Materials in computer graphics","x":-801,"y":-2,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"409c2036a65e9206","type":"text","text":"Soft body object","x":-2130,"y":627,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"5f7e707bf5adef7e","type":"text","text":"Material modeling is specifying mechanical material properties for geometry.\n\nThese properties are used during physical simulation of object behavior which is taking in account geometry, position, orientation, scale, velocity and forces.","x":-2320,"y":345,"width":640,"height":191},
		{"id":"25675e50bf7a81b1","type":"file","file":"Images/Material/Tools/Metal.jpg","x":-3566,"y":-2371,"width":259,"height":400},
		{"id":"82a4eaa7e341ab13","type":"file","file":"Images/Material/Tools/SSS_Skin.jpg","x":-2940,"y":-2274,"width":269,"height":240},
		{"id":"471109f6d86965c0","type":"text","text":"Translucent surfaces and SSS: wax, skin, paint, etc.","x":-3241,"y":-2421,"width":565,"height":80},
		{"id":"2fa3e838b87d77f4","type":"file","file":"Images/Material/Tools/SSS_Statue.jpg","x":-3236,"y":-1993,"width":253,"height":252},
		{"id":"01eff752068e86a4","type":"text","text":"Transparent surfaces: liquids, glass, wine, milk, etc.","x":-2638,"y":-2426,"width":400,"height":80},
		{"id":"743d29c4b0ef6649","type":"text","text":"In this lecture we will focus on modeling with volumes, that is, voxel grids.\n\nLater, we will return to modeling volume material.","x":-3671,"y":-1472,"width":731,"height":141},
		{"id":"e6d7c6ab296c307b","type":"text","text":"Blender Keywords:\n* Material\n* Shader nodes\n* Cycles\n* EEVEE","x":-3740,"y":240,"width":384,"height":220},
